<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <style name="Theme.RimBoard" parent="Theme.Material3.DayNight">
        <item name="preferenceTheme">@style/PreferenceThemeOverlay</item>
        <item name="colorPrimary">#1A73E8</item>
        <!--
            Overriding colorPrimary without this left the *label* on every
            filled button at Material 3's baseline, which in the dark theme is
            a dark purple (#381E72). Measured off a device screenshot of the
            setup screen: #381E72 on #1A73E8 is 2.92:1, well under the 4.5:1
            WCAG AA needs for text this size — three unreadable buttons on the
            first screen a new install shows. White is 4.51:1.

            The pair is the unit, not the colour: a theme that sets one half
            and inherits the other is asking two different design systems what
            the button should look like. ThemesTest holds them together.
        -->
        <item name="colorOnPrimary">#FFFFFF</item>
        <!--
            And the container half of the same family, for the same reason.
            Every switch in settings was drawing a Material 3 baseline purple
            track (#4F378B, M3's dark `primaryContainer`) around a RimBoard blue
            thumb — two design systems in one control, on a screen where nothing
            else is purple. `SwitchCompat` under a Material 3 theme tints its
            track from `colorPrimaryContainer` and its thumb from `colorPrimary`,
            so overriding only the latter split the control in half.

            The values are the app's own blues, not new ones: #0B47A1 is already
            the far end of the settings header gradient. #D3E3FD on it is 6.66:1.
        -->
        <item name="colorPrimaryContainer">#0B47A1</item>
        <item name="colorOnPrimaryContainer">#D3E3FD</item>
    </style>
</resources>
